          "type": "output_text",
          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"occurrence_id\":\"fontane-effi-briest-c9a7185a57602a96\",\"core_classification\":{\"label_support\":{\"truth_conditional\":4,\"performative\":0,\"exclamatory_reflexive\":0,\"other\":0},\"confidence\":0.97,\"analysis\":\"Effi directly presents her love for her parents as an established emotional fact—indeed, one so obvious that it supposedly need not be mentioned. The utterance neither undertakes a relational commitment nor functions as a reflex-like affective discharge; its rhetorical role in deflecting concern about her love for Geert does not alter its predominantly truth-conditional force.\",\"ambiguity\":null},\"other_diagnosis\":{\"tpe_failure\":null,\"core_not_context\":null},\"utterance_status\":{\"status\":\"direct\",\"description\":\"A directly spoken avowal by Effi, syntactically embedded in “daß ich euch liebe” and addressed through her mother to both parents.\"},\"contextual_interpretation\":\"Effi includes her parents among a broad catalogue of people she loves after her mother asks whether she loves Geert. Saying that her love for them goes without saying conveys secure familial affection, while the catalogue also dilutes the romantic significance of “love” and helps Effi evade the sharper question about her fiancé. Her subsequent admission that she fears Geert reinforces this evasive framing, but does not undermine the utterance’s presentation of parental love as true.\",\"evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"e1\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“Und daß ich euch liebe, davon spreche ich gar nicht erst.”\",\"supports\":\"The embedded clause explicitly avows Effi’s love for the plural addressees and presents it as too self-evident to require mention, strongly supporting T.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"e2\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Effi precedes and follows the clause with a catalogue of people she loves: “Ich liebe Hulda ... Bertha ... Hertha ... den alten Niemeyer” and “Ich liebe alle, die’s gut mit mir meinen.”\",\"supports\":\"The utterance is part of a reflective classification of her affectionate states rather than a commitment-making act or reflexive emotional discharge.\",\"confidence\":0.97},{\"evidence_id\":\"e3\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The exchange begins with Frau von Briest asking, “Liebst du Geert nicht?”, after which Effi generalizes love across friends, family, and kind people.\",\"supports\":\"The parental avowal is sincere-looking but also rhetorically embedded in Effi’s deflection of a question about romantic love for Geert.\",\"confidence\":0.96},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4\",\"source\":\"supplied_metadata\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The occurrence is marked affirmative, present-tense, subordinate, and syntactically embedded.\",\"supports\":\"Confirms that the classified event is an embedded but explicit affirmative love proposition within direct speech.\",\"confidence\":0.99}],\"background_knowledge\":{\"used\":false,\"familiarity\":\"none\",\"confidence\":null,\"contribution\":null},\"ontology_assessment\":{\"fit\":\"natural\",\"diagnosis\":\"T adequately captures the core avowal. Its familial, generalized, and evasive contextual functions require no additional core-force category beyond T/P/E.\",\"candidate_recurrent_dimension\":null},\"notes\":\"The plural “euch” most naturally refers to both parents, although Effi is immediately speaking with her mother.\"}"
        }
      ],
